    "lesson_order": 1,
    "title": "Understanding and representing integers",
    "summary": "Identify integers, opposites, and absolute value. Represent, compare, and order integers on a number line.",
    "skill_codes": [
      "S1",
      "S5"
    ],
    "content_schema_version": 1,
    "content": {
      "source_text": "Lesson 1 — Understanding and representing integers\nSkills: S1 + S5\nObjectives:\n1. Identify integers, opposites, and absolute value.\n2. Represent, compare, and order integers on a number line.\nWhat is an integer?\nAn integer is a whole number. It can be positive, negative, or zero. It has no fractional part and\nno decimal.\nExamples: -8, -3, 0, 5, 12.\nNot integers: 3/4, 0.6, 12%.\nWe use negative numbers every day. A temperature 3 degrees below zero is -3. A debt of 500\npesos is -500. A diver 6 meters under the sea is at -6.\nThe number line\nA number line shows integers in order. Zero is in the middle. Positive integers go right. Negative\nintegers go left.\nValues increase as you move right. So -7 < -2 < 0 < 4.\nCompare by position, not by digit size. -7 is smaller than -2 because it is farther left.\n[insert figure: lesson1/integer-number-line]\nOpposites and absolute value\nOpposites are two integers the same distance from zero on different sides. -5 and 5 are opposites.\nAbsolute value is the distance from zero. Distance is always positive or zero. We write it with\ntwo bars.\n-\n|5| = 5\n-\n|-5| = 5\n-\n|0| = 0\n[insert figure: lesson1/opposites-example]\n[insert video: VID-INTEGER-NUMBER-LINE | 1:00-1:50] (optional)\nWorked example\nProblem: Is -11 an integer? What is its opposite? What is |-11|?\nStep 1: -11 has no fraction and no decimal. So it is an integer.\nStep 2: The opposite of -11 is 11. Same distance, other side of zero.\nStep 3: |-11| = 11 because -11 is 11 units from zero.\nAnswer: -11 is an integer. Opposite: 11. Absolute value: 11.\nCommon mistake\n-7 > -3 is wrong. The digit 7 is bigger, but -7 is farther left. Correct: -7 < -3.\nReady to practice\nContinue to the regular exercise for 6 questions covering both skills."
    }
  },
